{"purpose":"Establish why walkie exists and what it must remain, so that every downstream requirement, decision, work item and runbook can be traced to a single statement of intent.","content":"## Vision\n\nwalkie is a terminal-native communication tool for a small team whose devices already share a Tailscale tailnet. It carries text, presence and voice between those devices without asking any of them to install a graphical environment.\n\n## Who it is for\n\nFewer than 20 devices — laptops, desktops and embedded systems — operated by a mix of technical and non-technical people. Some of those devices cannot install GUI dependencies at all. Some run on ARM. Some sit behind constrained links.\n\n## What it is for\n\n- Quick coordination inside the team, without leaving the terminal.\n- Emergency communication when the usual channels are unavailable.\n- File sharing during development work.\n- Remote troubleshooting and collaborative debugging.\n\n## Principles that constrain every downstream decision\n\n1. **The tailnet is the substrate.** walkie never reimplements what Tailscale already provides: peer identity, confidentiality in transit, NAT traversal, stable addressing. Duplicating any of these is treated as a defect, not a feature.\n2. **Terminal-first, no GUI dependency.** If a capability cannot be delivered in a terminal on a headless device, it is out of scope until it can.\n3. **Degrade rather than fail.** A device that cannot run the audio stack must remain completely useful for text, presence and files. Capability is per-device, not per-deployment.\n4. **Simple enough for non-technical users.** A working session must be reachable in no more than three commands, with no configuration file to hand-edit.\n5. **Small scale is a licence to stay simple.** Fewer than 20 devices means no sharding, no external message broker, no distributed consensus, no service mesh. Complexity must earn its place against this number.\n\n## Explicit non-goals\n\n- Not a replacement for a full team chat platform: no threads, no search across history, no integrations as a first-class concern.\n- Not a general-purpose remote-access tool. Remote shell access is Tailscale SSH's job.\n- Not a public or multi-tenant service. The trust boundary is one tailnet."}